<?xml version="1.0"?>
<sdf version="1.7">
  <model name="four_bar_unassembled">
    <!--
    A planar four-bar linkage described as an UNASSEMBLED loop of four joints.
    This is the same mechanism, in the same unassembled configuration, as the
    one that four_bar_auto.cc builds through the C++ API; here it comes from a
    file instead. See four_bar_unassembled.cc, which runs this model, and the
    README for more details.

    There are three moving links (driver, coupler, rocker) plus the ground link
    and four revolute joints. They are laid out below as they are defined,
    unassembled and conveniently lined up with the World frame axes. "*" marks
    the connection points, each of which is a frame on a link. The frame names
    start with a capital letter matching the link to which they are fixed and a
    lower case letter for the connected link, except that Do and Ro are monogram
    names for the driver and rocker link frame origins.

                                                      * Rc
                                                      |
                                                      |
                     coupler C                        |
     Cd *-------------------------- Co ------------------------* Cr
                            4.8m 1kg                  |
        * Dc                                          |
        |                                    rocker R | 2m
        |                                             | 2kg
    1m  | driver D                                    |
    1kg |                                             |      Wz
        |                                             |      |  Wy
        * Do                                       Ro *      | /
                                                             +----- Wx
        *====================== Go ===================*
       Wd         2m         ground G         2m       Wr

    In parent-child order, the joints connect Wd-Do, Wr-Ro, Dc-Cd and Cr-Rc.
    Link mass centers are at their midpoints. Lengths and masses are shown;
    inertias are those of thin rods (mass * length² / 12 about the mass
    center). All four revolute joints have their axes in the -y direction
    (towards you), so the mechanism moves in the World x-z plane and swings
    under Drake's usual -z gravity. The coupler's link frame Co sits at the
    middle of the bar rather than at either connection point.

    This configuration does not satisfy loop closure: Cr and Rc are apart.

    WHY THIS NEEDS DRAKE EXTENSIONS. A standard SDFormat joint has a single
    frame J, and Drake would place both of its joint frames there, one on each
    link. That makes every model parsed from a standard file assembled at
    q = 0, whatever poses its links are given. A Drake joint has two frames, Jp
    on the parent link and Jc on the child link, which coincide only when the
    joint is satisfied. So each joint below names them separately:

      <parent>, <child>    Which links the joint connects (standard SDFormat).
      <drake:parent_frame> The frame Jp, on the parent link.
      <drake:child_frame>  The frame Jc, on the child link.

    Each of the two must name an explicit <frame> fixed to the corresponding
    link, or that link itself. With both present, the joint ignores its <pose>,
    and its <axis> is resolved into Jc (Drake's joint axis has the same
    components in Jp and Jc). An axis without expressed_in, or one explicitly
    expressed in the joint frame, is interpreted directly in Jc. Each axis
    below is expressed in Jc directly.

    As a result, the joints no longer depend on where the links are posed.
    The link poses below draw the linkage as the figure does, but Drake does
    not use them: what Drake simulates is fixed entirely by the joints' frames.

    The <visual> elements are cosmetic only. They match four_bar_auto.cc: each
    link is a box running between its two connection points, stopping just
    short of them, and each joint is a translucent fat pin on its parent link
    around a thin pin on its child link. The one exception is the parent pin of
    the coupler_rocker joint. Drake will split the coupler to break the loop,
    move that joint to the coupler's "shadow", and draw the pin there instead.
    A model file cannot describe a link that does not exist until the model is
    finalized, so four_bar_unassembled.cc draws the shadow, and that pin.
    -->

    <!-- The ground "link" of the linkage, running from Wd to Wr. It is welded
    to World and carries no mass. It is a body of its own only because geometry
    must be attached to one, and a model file cannot put geometry on World. -->
    <link name="ground">
      <inertial>
        <mass>0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0</ixx> <iyy>0</iyy> <izz>0</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy> <ixz>0</ixz> <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="ground_bar">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>3.8 0.1 0.2</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0 1 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- The revolute axes are along y, while a cylinder's own axis is z, so
      the pins are rolled a quarter turn about x to match. -->
      <visual name="world_driver_parent_pin">
        <pose>-2 0 0 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.04</radius>
            <length>0.2</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0 1 0 0.5</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="world_rocker_parent_pin">
        <pose>2 0 0 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.04</radius>
            <length>0.2</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0 1 0 0.5</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="world_ground_weld" type="fixed">
      <parent>world</parent>
      <child>ground</child>
    </joint>
    <frame name="Wd" attached_to="ground">
      <pose relative_to="ground">-2 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </frame>
    <frame name="Wr" attached_to="ground">
      <pose relative_to="ground">2 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </frame>

    <!-- The driver, a thin rod from its link frame Do along +z to Dc. -->
    <link name="driver">
      <pose>-2 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>1</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0833333333333333</ixx>
          <iyy>0.0833333333333333</iyy>
          <izz>0</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy> <ixz>0</ixz> <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="driver_bar">
        <pose>0 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.2 0.1 0.8</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>1 0 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="world_driver_child_pin">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.02</radius>
            <length>0.2</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>1 0 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="driver_coupler_parent_pin">
        <pose>0 0 1 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.04</radius>
            <length>0.2</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>1 0 0 0.5</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <frame name="Dc" attached_to="driver">
      <pose relative_to="driver">0 0 1 0 0 0</pose>
    </frame>

    <!-- The coupler runs along x from Cd to Cr, with its link frame Co at the
    middle. Co is where the loop-closing weld constraint ends up, so it is
    drawn as a fat orange triad; the coupler's far end Cr is drawn as a thin
    blue one. -->
    <link name="coupler">
      <pose>0.4 0 1 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0</ixx>
          <iyy>1.92</iyy>
          <izz>1.92</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy> <ixz>0</ixz> <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="coupler_bar">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>4.6 0.1 0.2</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0 0 1 0.5</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="driver_coupler_child_pin">
        <pose>-2.4 0 0 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.02</radius>
            <length>0.2</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0 0 1 0.5</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- Each frame axis is a cylinder, rotated from z onto that axis. -->
      <visual name="Co_x_axis">
        <pose>0.125 0 0 0 1.5707963267948966 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.012</radius>
            <length>0.25</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0.833 0.333 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="Co_y_axis">
        <pose>0 0.125 0 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.012</radius>
            <length>0.25</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0.833 0.333 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="Co_z_axis">
        <pose>0 0 0.125 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.012</radius>
            <length>0.25</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0.833 0.333 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="Cr_x_axis">
        <pose>2.525 0 0 0 1.5707963267948966 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.006</radius>
            <length>0.25</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0 0 1 0.5</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="Cr_y_axis">
        <pose>2.4 0.125 0 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.006</radius>
            <length>0.25</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0 0 1 0.5</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="Cr_z_axis">
        <pose>2.4 0 0.125 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.006</radius>
            <length>0.25</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0 0 1 0.5</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <frame name="Cd" attached_to="coupler">
      <pose relative_to="coupler">-2.4 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </frame>
    <frame name="Cr" attached_to="coupler">
      <pose relative_to="coupler">2.4 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </frame>

    <!-- The rocker, a thin rod from its link frame Ro along +z to Rc. -->
    <link name="rocker">
      <pose>2 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 1 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>2</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.6666666666666667</ixx>
          <iyy>0.6666666666666667</iyy>
          <izz>0</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy> <ixz>0</ixz> <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="rocker_bar">
        <pose>0 0 1 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.2 0.1 1.8</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>1 1 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="world_rocker_child_pin">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.02</radius>
            <length>0.2</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>1 1 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="coupler_rocker_child_pin">
        <pose>0 0 2 1.5707963267948966 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.02</radius>
            <length>0.2</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>1 1 0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <frame name="Rc" attached_to="rocker">
      <pose relative_to="rocker">0 0 2 0 0 0</pose>
    </frame>

    <!-- The four revolute joints connect the frame pairs shown above, with
    q = 0 when each pair is coincident. Nothing here says anything about a
    spanning tree: the four joints simply form a loop, and MultibodyPlant
    decides how to model it. Only the first joint is actuated. -->

    <joint name="world_driver" type="revolute">
      <parent>ground</parent>
      <child>driver</child>
      <drake:parent_frame>Wd</drake:parent_frame>
      <drake:child_frame>driver</drake:child_frame>
      <axis>
        <xyz expressed_in="driver">0 -1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <!-- No effort limit provided. SDFormat defaults to -1, which we
               parse as an actuated joint. -->
        </limit>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <joint name="world_rocker" type="revolute">
      <parent>ground</parent>
      <child>rocker</child>
      <drake:parent_frame>Wr</drake:parent_frame>
      <drake:child_frame>rocker</drake:child_frame>
      <axis>
        <xyz expressed_in="rocker">0 -1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <!-- An effort of 0 is parsed as an unactuated joint. -->
          <effort>0</effort>
        </limit>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <joint name="driver_coupler" type="revolute">
      <parent>driver</parent>
      <child>coupler</child>
      <drake:parent_frame>Dc</drake:parent_frame>
      <drake:child_frame>Cd</drake:child_frame>
      <axis>
        <xyz expressed_in="Cd">0 -1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <effort>0</effort>
        </limit>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <joint name="coupler_rocker" type="revolute">
      <parent>coupler</parent>
      <child>rocker</child>
      <drake:parent_frame>Cr</drake:parent_frame>
      <drake:child_frame>Rc</drake:child_frame>
      <axis>
        <xyz expressed_in="Rc">0 -1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <effort>0</effort>
        </limit>
      </axis>
    </joint>

  </model>
</sdf>
